  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 405 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nFor Sunday, the upper ridge will continue to hold over the region, \nallowing more uncomfortable heat and humidity to persist over the \nregion. Recent ensemble guidance suggests slightly higher afternoon \ntemperatures in comparison to Saturday, with low to mid 90s in \nsouthern Indiana and north-central Kentucky, and mid-to-upper 90s \nover south-central Kentucky. HREF probabilities for heat indices at \nor above 105 increases to greater than 85% for our western CWA, \nthough chances for heat indices above 110 remain low (<15%). The \nHeat Advisory beginning later Saturday lasts through Sunday evening \nuntil 9 PM EDT/8 PM CDT. \n\nIn terms of chances for precipitation, the region will be in between \ntwo disturbances during the daytime hours on Sunday, especially \nsouth of the Ohio River. As a result, conditions will be mostly dry \nthrough most of the day before the frontal boundary of a disturbance \nover the NE CONUS pushes southeastward across the region late \nSunday into Sunday night. Ample instability will be in place out \nahead of the front (HREF mean CAPE > 2500 J/kg), though weaker wind \nshear (<30 kt of deep-layer shear) could limit the extent of \nstronger storm activity. The one thing that can counteract this some \nis that an area of >2\" PW will develop ahead of the front over \nsouthern Indiana and north-central Kentucky. While widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms are not expected late Sunday, isolated to \nwidely scattered showers and storms capable of producing heavier \ndownpours, gusty winds, and frequent lightning are possible in the \nnorthern CWA. The SPC has also put out a Slight Risk for severe \nweather for southern Indiana and north-central Kentucky due to this \npotential. \n\nOnce the cold front moves through Sunday night through Monday, weak \nsurface ridging and northerly surface flow will bring a much-needed \nreprieve from the hot and humid conditions we've been experiencing. \nOver the early portions of the workweek, dew points will decrease \nfrom the 70s to the 60s, with afternoon highs only in the mid-to-\nupper 80s. A few isolated showers and a rumble of thunder will be \npossible over southern Kentucky late Monday, with remaining chances \nof precipitation departing Monday night.\n\nFor Tuesday and most of Wednesday, surface ridging will keep \nconditions dry and temperatures near-normal for this time of year. \nLeading up to late Wednesday, a broader pattern shift will take \nplace. Across the CONUS, ridging over the west will amplify through \nthe end of the workweek as troughing digs over the Midwest/Ohio \nValley/NE CONUS. This will help set up a more unsettled pattern for \nour region, with slight chances for showers and storms Wednesday \nafternoon and lasting through the remainder of the workweek. \nTemperatures will remain at or below-normal during this stretch, \nallowing for a continued reprieve from uncomfortable heat and \nhumidity.",
